<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Gameplay</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            The gameplay kernel: hierarchical tags, definition ids that are a pure function of an
            address, the attribute modifier algebra with one fixed evaluation order, one effect type
            with a stacking policy, requirements, a reproducible random stream, and the module seam a
            game composes its rules out of. Definitions, state and rules — not a ruleset.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>gameplay;tags;attributes;effects;definitions;rpg;mmo;game-engine;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            ⚠ The shortness of this list is the design. docs/plan/28 § The dependency spine: every
            gameplay library depends on this one, so anything this one depends on is something every
            game that touches items or quests pays for. Vixen.Core is Symbol, the annotations and the
            service registry; Vixen.Ecs is here for the module seam alone — IGameplayModule declares
            systems and the phase they run in, and a phase is SystemPhase.

            Not Vixen.Engine, not Vixen.Assets, not Vixen.Net, and none of the three by accident. A
            definition is resolved through a registry this assembly declares an interface for and the
            host fills (ADR-013 gives Vixen.Assets the ref-counted handles); a DefId is a hash of a
            string, so nothing here needs the catalog to compute one; and what crosses the wire is the
            host's business, not the kernel's.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core\Vixen.Core.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Ecs\Vixen.Ecs.csproj" />

        <!--
            A definition is an artefact: the content build writes one and a player reads it back, so
            both halves need the generated serializer and the descriptor over the same types. The
            descriptor is the load-bearing half here — a `!ItemDefinition` tag in a .vxdef resolves
            through TypeRegistry's alias table, which is what the reflection generator emits.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Serialization\Vixen.Core.Serialization.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Reflection\Vixen.Core.Reflection.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Serialization.Generator\Vixen.Core.Serialization.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />

        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Reflection.Generator\Vixen.Core.Reflection.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Gameplay.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
